# netsurf: a style attribute change restyles locally, as a class change (patch 370)
# Generated 2026-11-01
# Base: seleron/vitabrowser (patch stack through 369)
#
# A fresh census of full-rebuild causes (r94, 21 sites; estimated cost =
# each site's mean reconvert time per batch) put nodeless/sheet batches
# first (doc 23, parked) and, unparked, the mutation kinds the local path
# refuses outright; a temporary trace split those: 10 of 14 were inline
# style attribute changes (el.style.x = ..., setAttribute('style')), on
# rte, stackoverflow, wpblog, cnnlite, google and youtube. Every one was a
# full box-tree rebuild: 89-109 ms on a 2500-paragraph sim page.
#
#  - macos9_reconvert.c: a SETATTR_STYLE mark goes to
#    html_reconvert_local_style() instead of the "kind" refusal.
#  - html.c: html_reconvert_local_style() treats the change as a class
#    change -- the element's own box selected afresh by the class paths:
#    the inline content of its block (165/173), a block restyle (174), a
#    cell's table (369); an element with no box before (display: none) is
#    met by its parent's rebuild as a new one. The current class is passed
#    as the "old" one, so patch 186's sibling check sees no class change.
#    Declined only when a sibling selector can test the attribute: a
#    left compound of + / ~ with [style...] in the patch-186 cache, or a
#    sheet that scan gave up on (state 2) whose text selects on style at
#    all (patch 310's scan). A [style] selector on the element or a
#    descendant is covered by the rebuild; :has() never matches here (328).
#  - html.c, html_rl_block_restyle (174, shared with class changes): a flex
#    or grid item, and a block whose siblings include flex, grid or table
#    boxes or float carriers, are restyled too, the page then laid out
#    whole -- the local placement was what needed plain blocks.
#
# Result on the sim page: a span's colour 97 -> 8 ms, a paragraph's margin
# 91 -> 7 ms, a cell's background 94 -> 17 ms, a flex item's flex-grow
# 94 -> 17 ms. Eleven steps, and four sheet shapes ([style] on self and
# descendants; [style] in a sibling rule; a state-2 sheet with and without
# [style]), render row for row as a parse-time build at every step.
#
